  "type": "answer",
  "content": "Cluster CL-SG-PRD-PAY is slowed by memory tiering on esxi-pay-05, not by generic capacity. The working set no longer fits the DRAM left on the host.\n\nMechanism\n* Memory tiering capacity is Active memory vs DRAM, not Consumed vs total. Worst ESXi Active Memory is 77 % of DRAM today vs 34-46 % Mon-Wed, and Highest ESXi DRAM Memory Consumed is 108 % – >100 % because the tier is counted in Consumed. `vsphere/technology/memory/metrics/virtual-motherboard/active`\n* Over the 3-day National Day weekend the host was idle: NVMe write bandwidth rose Friday 18:00 → Tuesday 08:00, NVMe consumed 0 → 280 GB, NVMe read bandwidth ~0. Pages went cold to NVMe.\n* Tuesday 09:10/10:15 NVMe read bandwidth is 380 / 360 MB/s sustained for 65 min, >200 MB/s threshold, with NVMe read latency ~290 µs. The decision tree step 3 flags sustained >200 MB/s as “working set no longer fits the DRAM left”. `vsphere/technology/memory/decision-tree/is-memory-tiering-slowing-host`\n* Effective DRAM is reduced by the non-tiered SQL VM. sql-pay-03 is 256 GB reserved, tiering off, active 110 GB on esxi-pay-05. A full reservation is a guarantee and cannot be overcommitted; admission control counts reservations, never usage. `vsphere/architecture/resource-controls` The reservation plus the active working set of the 34 tiered VMs pushes Active to 790 GB ≈ 77 % of 1 TB DRAM, so tier fetches are unavoidable.\n\nCluster Risk 66 %, Cluster Performance 48 % and the 09:05 latency spike line up with the NVMe wait burst. pay-api-07 shows VM Performance 17 % with 6 MB/s NVMe read, Memory Contention 0.2 % – the wait is tier-induced, not classic memory contention.\n\nWhat to do now\n* Immediate relief: evacuate the non-tiered reserved VM. vMotion sql-pay-03 off esxi-pay-05 to esxi-pay-07/08 which have Active 44/41 % of DRAM and only ~30 MB/s NVMe read. That frees ~256 GB of reserved DRAM for the tiered workload.\n* Meanwhile move the hottest tiered VMs off esxi-pay-05; start with the VMs contributing to the 380 MB/s NVMe read. Check per-VM NVMe read bandwidth `memstats -r vmtier-stats` and migrate to the cool hosts.\n* Keep ballooned/compressed/swapped at 0 – they are not the cause; NVMe wait is. Do not add memory or change tier size now; monitor NVMe read bandwidth for 1 hour after moves to confirm it falls <200 MB/s and latency drops.",
